window.__GUIDE_CHUNKS__.push([{"id": "06-002", "chapter_num": 6, "chapter_title": "The blacklist (seems smart, isn't)", "chapter_file": "06-the-blacklist.md", "number": 2, "title": "Do not pay for \"conversion therapy\" or send family members to it", "money": "0", "time": "low", "willpower": "no", "benefit": "high", "dimension": "freedom", "evidence": "B (disputed)", "grade": "B", "disputed": true, "cost": "Free. What is saved is whatever conversion programs, clinics and \"quit being gay\" camps charge. The hard part is when family members insist on sending someone.", "plain": "The American Psychological Association's task force reviewed the research and said these efforts are unlikely to change orientation and carry some risk of harm. In a US survey, people who had undergone them reported suicidal thoughts at nearly twice the rate. In Canada, providing conversion therapy is a criminal offence.", "benefit_text": "The APA's 2009 task-force report: efforts to change sexual orientation are \"unlikely to be successful and involve some risk of harm\", and it advised against parents considering forced or residential programs for minors. A US survey of 1,518 sexual-minority adults: about 7% had undergone change efforts, four-fifths from religious figures. After adjusting for age and childhood adversity, those who had undergone them had lifetime suicidal ideation at OR 1.92 (95% CI 1.01–3.64) — nearly double. Another study of 245 young adults aged 21 to 25: 22.0% of those never subjected to change efforts had attempted suicide, versus 48.1% where parents had tried to change them and 62.8% where parents had also sent them to a therapist or religious figure. The WHO's ICD-10 states: \"sexual orientation by itself is not to be regarded as a disorder.\" In Canada, Criminal Code sections 320.101–320.104, in force January 7, 2022: causing or providing conversion therapy carries up to 5 years (s. 320.102); promoting, advertising or profiting from it carries up to 2 years (ss. 320.103–320.104).", "sources": "American Psychological Association Task Force on Appropriate Therapeutic Responses to Sexual Orientation (2009). Report of the Task Force on Appropriate Therapeutic Responses to Sexual Orientation. <https://www.apa.org/pi/lgbt/resources/therapeutic-response.pdf>; Blosnich JR, Henderson ER, Coulter RWS, Goldbach JT, Meyer IH. (2020). Sexual Orientation Change Efforts, Adverse Childhood Experiences, and Suicide Ideation and Attempt Among Sexual Minority Adults, United States, 2016–2018. American Journal of Public Health, 110(7), 1024–1030. <https://doi.org/10.2105/AJPH.2020.305637>; Ryan C, Toomey RB, Diaz RM, Russell ST. (2020). Parent-Initiated Sexual Orientation Change Efforts With LGBT Adolescents: Implications for Young Adult Mental Health and Adjustment. Journal of Homosexuality, 67(2), 159–173. <https://doi.org/10.1080/00918369.2018.1538407>; World Health Organization. ICD-10 Version:2019, F66 Psychological and behavioural disorders associated with sexual development and orientation. <https://icd.who.int/browse10/2019/en#/F66>; Criminal Code, R.S.C. 1985, c. C-46, ss. 320.101–320.104 (conversion therapy offences, in force January 7, 2022 per Department of Justice): definition <https://laws-lois.justice.gc.ca/eng/acts/c-46/section-320.101.html>; causing/providing, up to 5 years (s. 320.102) <https://laws-lois.justice.gc.ca/eng/acts/c-46/section-320.102.html>; promoting/advertising, up to 2 years (s. 320.103) <https://laws-lois.justice.gc.ca/eng/acts/c-46/section-320.103.html>; material benefit, up to 2 years (s. 320.104) <https://laws-lois.justice.gc.ca/eng/acts/c-46/section-320.104.html>; in-force date <https://www.justice.gc.ca/eng/rp-pr/jr/ct-tc/p1.html>; Sullins DP. (2022). Sexual Orientation Change Efforts Do Not Increase Suicide: Correcting a False Research Narrative. Archives of Sexual Behavior, 51, 3377–3393. <https://doi.org/10.1007/s10508-022-02408-2>; Blosnich JR, Coulter RWS, Henderson ER, et al. (2023). Correcting a False Research Narrative: A Commentary on Sullins (2022). Archives of Sexual Behavior, 52(3), 885–888. <https://doi.org/10.1007/s10508-022-02521-2>", "notes": "Disputed. Sullins (2022) re-ran the same survey data excluding suicidal thoughts that predated the change efforts, and the positive link disappeared (OR 0.44, 0.20–0.94). Sullins's institution opposes homosexuality, and the original authors published a reply — so how large the harm is remains disputed. What has no counter-evidence is that it does not work. The B grade is because the harm numbers come from retrospective surveys. If you are in lasting pain over this, see a psychiatrist or clinical psychologist with the goal of easing the pain, not changing the orientation.", "urls": ["https://doi.org/10.1007/s10508-022-02408-2", "https://doi.org/10.1007/s10508-022-02521-2", "https://doi.org/10.1080/00918369.2018.1538407", "https://doi.org/10.2105/AJPH.2020.305637", "https://icd.who.int/browse10/2019/en#/F66", "https://laws-lois.justice.gc.ca/eng/acts/c-46/section-320.101.html", "https://laws-lois.justice.gc.ca/eng/acts/c-46/section-320.102.html", "https://laws-lois.justice.gc.ca/eng/acts/c-46/section-320.103.html", "https://laws-lois.justice.gc.ca/eng/acts/c-46/section-320.104.html", "https://www.apa.org/pi/lgbt/resources/therapeutic-response.pdf", "https://www.justice.gc.ca/eng/rp-pr/jr/ct-tc/p1.html"]}, {"id": "06-003", "chapter_num": 6, "chapter_title": "The blacklist (seems smart, isn't)", "chapter_file": "06-the-blacklist.md", "number": 3, "title": "Do not take a multivitamin for longevity or heart protection", "money": "0", "time": "low", "willpower": "no", "benefit": "mid", "dimension": "money", "evidence": "A (disputed)", "grade": "A", "disputed": true, "cost": "About $70–250 a year. Swallowing it takes no time, one pill. Long use breeds a dependency of its own: skipping a day feels like skipping something.", "plain": "Fourteen thousand male physicians took a multivitamin for over eleven years. Major cardiovascular events and deaths were the same as without it. Pooling nine trials and over fifty thousand people, no link to total mortality either. Not buying saves the yearly spend. People with a diagnosed deficiency, and pregnant people taking folic acid, are not covered here.", "benefit_text": "The PHS II trial: 14,641 male physicians, median 11.2 years. Takers and non-takers had the same rate of major cardiovascular events: HR 1.01 (95% CI 0.91–1.10). Total mortality HR 0.94 (0.88–1.02) — no difference. The US Preventive Services Task Force (2022) pooled 9 randomized trials, 51,550 people: no link between multivitamins and total mortality. Grade I — evidence insufficient to say.", "sources": "Sesso HD et al. (2012). Multivitamins in the prevention of cardiovascular disease in men: the Physicians' Health Study II randomized controlled trial. JAMA. <https://doi.org/10.1001/jama.2012.14805>; US Preventive Services Task Force (2022). Vitamin, Mineral, and Multivitamin Supplementation to Prevent Cardiovascular Disease and Cancer. JAMA. <https://doi.org/10.1001/jama.2022.8970>; Gaziano JM et al. (2012). Multivitamins in the Prevention of Cancer in Men. JAMA. <https://doi.org/10.1001/jama.2012.14641>", "notes": "Disputed. In the same physicians, cancer was also tracked: total cancer was about 8% lower in takers (0.86–0.998, P = 0.04 — just crossing the \"not by chance\" line), cancer death about 12% lower (0.77–1.01, crossing 1, so possibly chance). The benefit is small and seen only in male physicians. People with a diagnosed deficiency, pregnant people (folic acid), and people who eat very poorly are excluded — supplement as your doctor says. The price range is approximate 2026 Canadian retail, UNVERIFIED — see docs/verification/06-the-blacklist.md.", "urls": ["https://doi.org/10.1001/jama.2012.14641", "https://doi.org/10.1001/jama.2012.14805", "https://doi.org/10.1001/jama.2022.8970"]}, {"id": "06-004", "chapter_num": 6, "chapter_title": "The blacklist (seems smart, isn't)", "chapter_file": "06-the-blacklist.md", "number": 4, "title": "Do not take ordinary fish oil to prevent cardiovascular disease", "money": "0", "time": "low", "willpower": "no", "benefit": "mid", "dimension": "money", "evidence": "A (disputed)", "grade": "A", "disputed": true, "cost": "About $100–350 a year. Swallowing it takes no time.", "plain": "Two large trials — twenty-six thousand people and fifteen thousand patients with diabetes — gave one gram of fish oil a day. Major cardiovascular events and deaths were the same as without it. \"Eating fish is good\" does not equal \"fish-oil capsules are good.\"", "benefit_text": "The VITAL trial: 25,871 people, 1 g a day, median 5.3 years. Major cardiovascular events HR 0.92 (95% CI 0.80–1.06 — crossing 1, no difference). Total mortality HR 1.02 (0.90–1.15). The ASCEND trial: 15,480 patients with diabetes, 1 g a day, mean 7.4 years, also no difference: serious vascular events RR 0.97 (0.87–1.08), total mortality RR 0.95 (0.86–1.05).", "sources": "Manson JE et al. (2019). Marine n-3 Fatty Acids and Prevention of Cardiovascular Disease and Cancer. NEJM. <https://doi.org/10.1056/NEJMoa1811403>; ASCEND Study Collaborative Group (2018). Effects of n-3 Fatty Acid Supplements in Diabetes Mellitus. NEJM. <https://doi.org/10.1056/NEJMoa1804989>; Bhatt DL et al. (2019). Cardiovascular Risk Reduction with Icosapent Ethyl for Hypertriglyceridemia. New England Journal of Medicine. <https://doi.org/10.1056/NEJMoa1812792>", "notes": "Disputed. One trial did work: REDUCE-IT, 8,179 people who already had cardiovascular disease or diabetes, all on statins, with high triglycerides (135–499 mg/dL). They took 4 g a day of prescription pure EPA: events 17.2% versus 22.0%, about a quarter lower (HR 0.75, 0.68–0.83). But that is a high-dose prescription drug for high-risk patients, not a supplement for ordinary people. Eating fish itself is not covered here. The price range is approximate 2026 Canadian retail, UNVERIFIED — see docs/verification/06-the-blacklist.md.", "urls": ["https://doi.org/10.1056/NEJMoa1804989", "https://doi.org/10.1056/NEJMoa1811403", "https://doi.org/10.1056/NEJMoa1812792"]}, {"id": "06-005", "chapter_num": 6, "chapter_title": "The blacklist (seems smart, isn't)", "chapter_file": "06-the-blacklist.md", "number": 5, "title": "Do not give vitamin D to people who are not deficient, for longevity", "money": "0", "time": "low", "willpower": "no", "benefit": "mid", "dimension": "money", "evidence": "A (disputed)", "grade": "A", "disputed": true, "cost": "About $20–50 a year. The money is small; the real cost is believing you have done your prevention.", "plain": "People who were not deficient took vitamin D for five years. Cancer, cardiovascular disease and deaths were the same as without it. People with a diagnosed deficiency, people with osteoporosis, people with no sun exposure are not covered here — they supplement as their doctor says.", "benefit_text": "The VITAL trial: 25,871 people, 2,000 IU a day, median 5.3 years. No difference in cancer, cardiovascular events or mortality. Metastatic or fatal cancer HR 0.96 (95% CI 0.88–1.06); major cardiovascular events HR 0.97 (0.85–1.12); total mortality HR 0.99 (0.87–1.12). The D-Health trial: 21,315 Australians aged 60 and up, 60,000 IU a month, 5 years — also no difference: total mortality HR 1.04 (0.93–1.18), deaths 5.3% versus 5.1%.", "sources": "Manson JE et al. (2019). Vitamin D Supplements and Prevention of Cancer and Cardiovascular Disease. NEJM. <https://doi.org/10.1056/NEJMoa1809944>; Neale RE et al. (2022). The D-Health Trial: a randomised controlled trial of the effect of vitamin D on mortality. Lancet Diabetes Endocrinol. <https://doi.org/10.1016/S2213-8587(21)00345-4>", "notes": "Disputed. Neither trial recruited people who were actually deficient, so the conclusion only covers people who were not deficient to begin with. People with a diagnosed deficiency, osteoporosis, no sun exposure, and infants still supplement as their doctor says. Only total mortality, cancer and cardiovascular outcomes were looked at; other outcomes were not. The price range is approximate 2026 Canadian retail, UNVERIFIED — see docs/verification/06-the-blacklist.md.", "urls": ["https://doi.org/10.1016/S2213-8587(21", "https://doi.org/10.1056/NEJMoa1809944"]}, {"id": "06-006", "chapter_num": 6, "chapter_title": "The blacklist (seems smart, isn't)", "chapter_file": "06-the-blacklist.md", "number": 6, "title": "Do not take antioxidant supplements (beta-carotene, vitamin E, vitamin A) to prevent cancer", "money": "0", "time": "low", "willpower": "no", "benefit": "mid", "dimension": "death", "evidence": "A", "grade": "A", "disputed": false, "cost": "About $50–200 a year. For smokers, the real cost is lung-cancer risk going up with it.", "plain": "This one is not just useless. Pooling seventy-eight trials and nearly three hundred thousand people, takers had slightly higher mortality. In two large trials of smokers, beta-carotene raised lung-cancer rates by about a fifth to a quarter, and deaths by about a tenth. Smokers and people exposed to asbestos especially should not take it.", "benefit_text": "Cochrane pooled 78 randomized trials, 296,707 people. Takers of antioxidant supplements: total mortality RR 1.02 (95% CI 0.98–1.05); in the better-designed, less biased trials, RR 1.04 (1.01–1.07) — slightly higher. Beta-carotene alone RR 1.05 (1.01–1.09); vitamin E alone RR 1.03 (1.00–1.05). The ATBC trial: 29,133 male smokers, 20 mg a day — lung cancer up 18% (3%–36%), total mortality up 8% (1%–16%). The CARET trial: 18,314 smokers or asbestos-exposed people — lung-cancer risk up about 28% (RR 1.28, 1.04–1.57), total mortality up about 17% (RR 1.17, 1.03–1.33).", "sources": "Bjelakovic G et al. (2012). Antioxidant supplements for prevention of mortality in healthy participants and patients with various diseases. Cochrane Database Syst Rev. <https://doi.org/10.1002/14651858.CD007176.pub2>; The Alpha-Tocopherol, Beta Carotene Cancer Prevention Study Group (1994). The effect of vitamin E and beta carotene on the incidence of lung cancer and other cancers in male smokers. NEJM. <https://doi.org/10.1056/NEJM199404143301501>; Omenn GS et al. (1996). Effects of a combination of beta carotene and vitamin A on lung cancer and cardiovascular disease. NEJM. <https://doi.org/10.1056/NEJM199605023341802>", "notes": "The USPSTF gave beta-carotene and vitamin E a D grade in 2022 — do not use. Smokers and asbestos-exposed people have already been harmed by this. Antioxidants that come in vegetables and fruit are not covered — keep eating those. The price range is approximate 2026 Canadian retail, UNVERIFIED — see docs/verification/06-the-blacklist.md.", "urls": ["https://doi.org/10.1002/14651858.CD007176.pub2", "https://doi.org/10.1056/NEJM199404143301501", "https://doi.org/10.1056/NEJM199605023341802"]}, {"id": "06-007", "chapter_num": 6, "chapter_title": "The blacklist (seems smart, isn't)", "chapter_file": "06-the-blacklist.md", "number": 7, "title": "Do not expect glucosamine/chondroitin to treat knee arthritis", "money": "0", "time": "low", "willpower": "no", "benefit": "mid", "dimension": "money", "evidence": "A (disputed)", "grade": "A", "disputed": true, "cost": "About $250–600 a year.", "plain": "Knee-arthritis patients took a placebo for twenty-four weeks, and six in ten said the pain eased. Glucosamine, and chondroitin, added only four to five percentage points — small enough to be chance. The yearly spend buys mostly that six-in-ten placebo effect.", "benefit_text": "The GAIT trial: 1,583 knee-osteoarthritis patients, 24 weeks. 60.1% of placebo takers reported pain relief. Glucosamine beat that by 3.9 percentage points (P = 0.30); chondroitin by 5.3 (P = 0.17); both together by 6.5 (P = 0.09) — all too small to rule out chance. The real drug used as control, celecoxib, beat placebo by 10.0 points (P = 0.008).", "sources": "Clegg DO et al. (2006). Glucosamine, chondroitin sulfate, and the two in combination for painful knee osteoarthritis. NEJM. <https://doi.org/10.1056/NEJMoa052771>", "notes": "Disputed. In the same trial, among patients who started with severe pain, the combination worked for 79.2% versus 54.3% on placebo (P = 0.002) — but the authors call this a post-hoc finding, a lead only. What actually has evidence for knee arthritis is weight loss and muscle training — see the \"Don't die slowly\" chapter. The price range is approximate 2026 Canadian retail, UNVERIFIED — see docs/verification/06-the-blacklist.md.", "urls": ["https://doi.org/10.1056/NEJMoa052771"]}, {"id": "06-008", "chapter_num": 6, "chapter_title": "The blacklist (seems smart, isn't)", "chapter_file": "06-the-blacklist.md", "number": 8, "title": "Do not rely on vitamin C to prevent colds", "money": "0", "time": "low", "willpower": "no", "benefit": "low", "dimension": "money", "evidence": "A (disputed)", "grade": "A", "disputed": true, "cost": "About $100–250 a year.", "plain": "Taking vitamin C daily barely changes how often ordinary people catch colds, and starting it after catching one works inconsistently. Daily use shortens one cold by about eight percent — half a day less of misery. Whether that is worth the yearly spend is your call. Marathon runners and soldiers marching in the cold are the exception: for them, colds were cut by about half.", "benefit_text": "Cochrane pooled 29 trials, 11,306 people. Ordinary people taking vitamin C daily: colds barely fewer, RR 0.97 (95% CI 0.94–1.00). Starting after catching a cold: \"no consistent effect\" — some trials worked, some did not. The same review: long-term daily use shortened colds by about 8% in adults (95% CI 3%–12%) and 14% in children (7%–21%). The exception: 5 trials, 598 marathon runners, skiers and soldiers in cold regions — colds cut by about half, RR 0.48 (0.35–0.64).", "sources": "Hemilä H, Chalker E (2013). Vitamin C for preventing and treating the common cold. Cochrane Database Syst Rev. <https://doi.org/10.1002/14651858.CD000980.pub4>", "notes": "Disputed. The same review confirms daily use shortens colds: about 8% in adults (3%–12%), 14% in children (7%–21%) — roughly half a day per cold. Marathon runners, skiers and soldiers marching in the cold are the exception: 5 trials, 598 people, about half as many colds (RR 0.48, 0.35–0.64). Almost nobody belongs to those groups. The price range is approximate 2026 Canadian retail, UNVERIFIED — see docs/verification/06-the-blacklist.md.", "urls": ["https://doi.org/10.1002/14651858.CD000980.pub4"]}, {"id": "06-009", "chapter_num": 6, "chapter_title": "The blacklist (seems smart, isn't)", "chapter_file": "06-the-blacklist.md", "number": 9, "title": "Do not get a \"whole-body PET-CT\" or a \"tumour-marker panel\" when you have no symptoms", "money": "0", "time": "low", "willpower": "no", "benefit": "mid", "dimension": "death", "evidence": "A", "grade": "A", "disputed": false, "cost": "A private whole-body scan costs a few thousand dollars, plus one dose of ionizing radiation. A tumour-marker panel costs a few hundred. The real cost comes later: one \"abnormality\" that turns out to be a false alarm, then the follow-up scans, biopsies and surgery — the money and the suffering sit in that stretch.", "plain": "Screening people with no symptoms mostly finds false alarms. Over eight in ten people scanned by whole-body CT had at least one \"abnormality\"; nearly four in ten were called back, most of them benign. Women screened for ovarian cancer with markers died of it slightly more often, and some had surgery for a false alarm.", "benefit_text": "The USPSTF gave screening symptom-free women for ovarian cancer with CA-125 and ultrasound a D grade — do not do it — based on the PLCO trial. Ovarian-cancer deaths: 0.34% screened versus 0.29% usual care, RR 1.18 (95% CI 0.82–1.71) — no benefit. Surgery for a false positive: 0.2%–3.25% across trials, up to 15% of them with serious surgical complications. Another study gave whole-body CT screening to 1,192 symptom-free people: 86% had at least one \"abnormality\", 37% were advised to get further checks — \"most findings benign as described\".", "sources": "US Preventive Services Task Force (2018). Screening for Ovarian Cancer: US Preventive Services Task Force Recommendation Statement. JAMA <https://doi.org/10.1001/jama.2017.21926>; US Preventive Services Task Force (2018). Screening for Ovarian Cancer: Recommendation Statement (official USPSTF page). <https://www.uspreventiveservicestaskforce.org/uspstf/recommendation/ovarian-cancer-screening>; Furtado CD et al. (2005). Whole-body CT screening: spectrum of findings and recommendations in 1192 patients. Radiology. <https://doi.org/10.1148/radiol.2372041741>", "notes": "The tumour-marker part rests on a randomized trial. The whole-body CT/PET-CT part rests only on follow-up records of people who were scanned — no control group — so it is a grade weaker, effectively B. The few screenings that do have evidence — colorectal, cervical, breast, low-dose chest CT for high-risk people — are a different matter; see the \"Don't die young\" chapter. People who already have symptoms, or already have cancer, getting a PET-CT are being treated, not screened — not covered here. The price range is approximate 2026 Canadian retail, UNVERIFIED — see docs/verification/06-the-blacklist.md.", "urls": ["https://doi.org/10.1001/jama.2017.21926", "https://doi.org/10.1148/radiol.2372041741", "https://www.uspreventiveservicestaskforce.org/uspstf/recommendation/ovarian-cancer-screening"]}, {"id": "06-010", "chapter_num": 6, "chapter_title": "The blacklist (seems smart, isn't)", "chapter_file": "06-the-blacklist.md", "number": 10, "title": "Do not expect a fitness band or watch to lose the weight for you", "money": "0", "time": "low", "willpower": "no", "benefit": "mid", "dimension": "money", "evidence": "B", "grade": "B", "disputed": false, "cost": "About $100–500 for the device, plus a little daily attention stolen by the numbers.", "plain": "Overweight people on the same diet-and-exercise plan: the band wearers lost three and a half kilos in two years; the non-wearers lost almost six. For weight loss, the device is not money that needs spending — exercise itself is what works.", "benefit_text": "The IDEA trial: 471 overweight or obese adults, 24 months, same diet and exercise plan, differing only in whether they wore a tracker. Wearers lost 3.5 kg (95% CI 2.6–4.5); non-wearers lost 5.9 kg (5.0–6.8) — more. Difference 2.4 kg (1.0–3.7, P = 0.002).", "sources": "Jakicic JM et al. (2016). Effect of Wearable Technology Combined With a Lifestyle Intervention on Long-term Weight Loss: The IDEA Randomized Clinical Trial. JAMA. <https://doi.org/10.1001/jama.2016.12858>", "notes": "One trial only, using early-2010s arm-worn devices — this does not say \"all wearables are useless\". Exercise itself is effective; see the \"Don't die slowly\" chapter. The conclusion is one sentence: for weight loss, the device is not money you need to spend. The price range is approximate 2026 Canadian retail, UNVERIFIED — see docs/verification/06-the-blacklist.md.", "urls": ["https://doi.org/10.1001/jama.2016.12858"]}, {"id": "06-011", "chapter_num": 6, "chapter_title": "The blacklist (seems smart, isn't)", "chapter_file": "06-the-blacklist.md", "number": 11, "title": "Do not pay a premium for organic food \"for health\"", "money": "0", "time": "low", "willpower": "no", "benefit": "mid", "dimension": "money", "evidence": "B", "grade": "B", "disputed": false, "cost": "Usually 1 to 3 times the price of the conventional equivalent.", "plain": "There is no strong evidence that organic food is more nutritious. Only three studies looked at what happened to people's health after eating it, and none found a difference. Organic produce does test positive for pesticide residues less often — by thirty percentage points — but detected does not mean over the limit. Eating more fruit and vegetables works either way.", "benefit_text": "A systematic review combined 17 human studies and 223 food-composition studies: \"lacking strong evidence that organic foods are significantly more nutritious than conventional foods\". Only 3 studies looked at health outcomes after eating (allergies and symptomatic infections) — no difference found. Organic produce tested positive for pesticide residues 30 percentage points less often — but detected is not over the limit.", "sources": "Smith-Spangler C et al. (2012). Are organic foods safer or healthier than conventional alternatives?: a systematic review. Ann Intern Med. <https://doi.org/10.7326/0003-4819-157-5-201209040-00007>", "notes": "Studies directly measuring health after eating are very few. So the conclusion is \"no evidence it is better\", not \"proven no benefit\". Environmental impact, animal welfare and taste are not covered here. Eating more fruit and vegetables is effective either way — organic or not.", "urls": ["https://doi.org/10.7326/0003-4819-157-5-201209040-00007"]}, {"id": "06-012", "chapter_num": 6, "chapter_title": "The blacklist (seems smart, isn't)", "chapter_file": "06-the-blacklist.md", "number": 12, "title": "Do not spend big on supplements, tonics or \"body-regulating\" gift boxes", "money": "0", "time": "low", "willpower": "no", "benefit": "high", "dimension": "money", "evidence": "C", "grade": "C", "disputed": false, "cost": "Hundreds to thousands of dollars. In gift packaging, the price often multiplies.", "plain": "In Canada, every natural health product needs a pre-market licence and an 8-digit product number (NPN) on the label, and its health claims must be evidence-backed — Health Canada's rule. The common ingredients — multivitamins, fish oil, vitamin D — have each been checked earlier in this chapter and none showed an effect. This money is usually spent on parents, carried through someone's door as a gift; the sentiment is fine, the product is the wrong pick.", "benefit_text": "Health Canada requires natural health products to hold a product licence before sale and to carry an 8-digit Natural Product Number (NPN) on the label; health claims must be supported by evidence. The regulator does not treat these products as medicines. The most common ingredients — multivitamins, fish oil, vitamin D, antioxidants, glucosamine, vitamin C — have each been checked against randomized trials in items 3 through 8 of this chapter.", "sources": "Health Canada. Natural health products: pre-market licensing and the NPN. <https://www.canada.ca/en/health-canada/services/drugs-health-products/natural-non-prescription/regulation/information-kit.html>", "notes": "The conclusion is \"no supporting evidence\", not \"proven ineffective\" — hence C. For products with clearly listed ingredients, check the matching item above. People with a diagnosed deficiency, or being treated for something, supplement as their doctor says. What is ruled out is the product, not the sentiment. If your parents already bought some and are taking it: as long as it is not replacing their prescribed drugs and causing no problems, this is not a fight worth having at the door — getting the vaccines and screenings done matters more. The same money buys things this guide can point to: the annual flu shot, the shingles vaccine after 50, the pneumococcal vaccine after 65, a blood-pressure cuff with the pills taken as prescribed, fall-proofing the bathroom and stairs, and the evidence-backed cancer screenings — all in the \"Don't die young\" chapter. As a gift, fruit, groceries, or food they actually like beats a tonic box. Two kinds are worth actively blocking: pay-first \"investment retirement\" schemes, and anything that would replace their medicine. The price range is approximate 2026 Canadian retail, UNVERIFIED — see docs/verification/06-the-blacklist.md.", "urls": ["https://www.canada.ca/en/health-canada/services/drugs-health-products/natural-non-prescription/regulation/information-kit.html"]}, {"id": "06-013", "chapter_num": 6, "chapter_title": "The blacklist (seems smart, isn't)", "chapter_file": "06-the-blacklist.md", "number": 13, "title": "Do not take probiotics long-term with no gut problems", "money": "0", "time": "low", "willpower": "no", "benefit": "low", "dimension": "money", "evidence": "B", "grade": "B", "disputed": false, "cost": "About $250–600 a year.", "plain": "Pooling forty-five trials in healthy adults, probiotics did not durably change the gut flora — it reverted after stopping — and did not improve blood lipids. Antibiotic-caused diarrhoea and a few specific strain-for-disease uses have their own evidence and are not covered here.", "benefit_text": "A review pooled 45 trials in healthy adults: \"failed to support that probiotics cause lasting changes in gut microbiota, nor improve blood lipids in healthy adults\". The flora did shift, but only temporarily — it reverted after stopping.", "sources": "Khalesi S et al. (2019). A review of probiotic supplementation in healthy adults: helpful or hype? Eur J Clin Nutr. <https://doi.org/10.1038/s41430-018-0135-9>", "notes": "The same review notes small improvements in healthy people: better-formed stools, more lactobacilli in women. Antibiotic-associated diarrhoea, and one specific strain for one specific disease, each have separate evidence — not covered here. This item targets healthy people taking it daily as a tonic. The price range is approximate 2026 Canadian retail, UNVERIFIED — see docs/verification/06-the-blacklist.md.", "urls": ["https://doi.org/10.1038/s41430-018-0135-9"]}, {"id": "06-014", "chapter_num": 6, "chapter_title": "The blacklist (seems smart, isn't)", "chapter_file": "06-the-blacklist.md", "number": 14, "title": "Do not force yourself into cold showers to \"boost immunity\"", "money": "0", "time": "low", "willpower": "no", "benefit": "low", "dimension": "money", "evidence": "B (disputed)", "grade": "B", "disputed": true, "cost": "Free. Thirty to ninety seconds of misery a day; in winter it takes resolve to keep up. People with heart disease should be careful — cold shock is a risk for them.", "plain": "In a three-thousand-person trial, cold-shower takers called in sick fewer times — about a third fewer — but their total sick days were the same, so it is not really less illness. Pooling eleven trials, inflammation markers briefly rose after cold water, and immunity markers did not move. Shower cold if you like it; just do not treat it as a health investment.", "benefit_text": "A Dutch randomized trial, 3,018 people, 30, 60 or 90 seconds of cold water after a hot shower. Sick-leave episodes fell 29% (IRR 0.71) — but the authors state \"no significant between-group difference in sick-leave days\": total days ill were the same. Quality of life, work productivity and anxiety showed no meaningful difference. A 2025 systematic review pooled 11 randomized trials, 3,177 people: inflammation markers rose right after and one hour after cold water; immunity markers did not change; stress fell only at the 12-hour mark. The authors call the evidence limited by \"few RCTs, small samples\".", "sources": "Buijze GA et al. (2016). The Effect of Cold Showering on Health and Work: A Randomized Controlled Trial. PLOS ONE. <https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0161749>; Cain T et al. (2025). Effects of cold-water immersion on health and wellbeing: A systematic review and meta-analysis. PLOS ONE. <https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0317615>", "notes": "Disputed. The 29% fewer sick-leave episodes is unlikely to be chance. But total sick days did not fall — it looks more like \"ill but showing up anyway\" than actually less illness. Athletes using cold water for post-training recovery are a different question, not covered here. Cold showers are not harmful to healthy people; just do not treat them as a health investment.", "urls": ["https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0161749", "https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0317615"]}, {"id": "06-015", "chapter_num": 6, "chapter_title": "The blacklist (seems smart, isn't)", "chapter_file": "06-the-blacklist.md", "number": 15, "title": "Do not buy detox, colon-cleanse, enzyme or alkaline-water products", "money": "0", "time": "low", "willpower": "no", "benefit": "mid", "dimension": "money", "evidence": "C", "grade": "C", "disputed": false, "cost": "Hundreds of dollars. The starve-yourself kind of \"detox\" can also tank blood sugar and nutrition.", "plain": "The detox industry is huge, but clinical evidence supporting it is tiny, and no one has run a proper trial of a commercial detox diet. One review hunted for a link between dietary acid load, alkaline water and cancer: of eight thousand papers, one qualified — and it found no link.", "benefit_text": "One review's conclusion: \"despite the popularity of the detox industry, there is very little clinical evidence to support these diets\", and \"no randomized controlled trials of commercial detox diets\". Another systematic review on dietary acid load, alkaline water and cancer: 8,278 papers screened, 1 qualified, no association found. The authors wrote that promoting alkaline diets and water for cancer prevention or treatment is \"not justified\".", "sources": "Klein AV, Kiat H (2015). Detox diets for toxin elimination and weight management: a critical review of the evidence. J Hum Nutr Diet. <https://doi.org/10.1111/jhn.12286>; Fenton TR, Huang T (2016). Systematic review of the association between dietary acid load, alkaline water and cancer. BMJ Open. <https://doi.org/10.1136/bmjopen-2015-010438>", "notes": "Only \"no supporting evidence\" can be said — hence C. Clearing metabolic waste is the liver's and kidneys' job; as long as those organs are healthy, no extra \"detox\" is needed. The price range is approximate 2026 Canadian retail, UNVERIFIED — see docs/verification/06-the-blacklist.md.", "urls": ["https://doi.org/10.1111/jhn.12286", "https://doi.org/10.1136/bmjopen-2015-010438"]}, {"id": "06-016", "chapter_num": 6, "chapter_title": "The blacklist (seems smart, isn't)", "chapter_file": "06-the-blacklist.md", "number": 16, "title": "Do not force down \"8 glasses a day\"", "money": "0", "time": "low", "willpower": "no", "benefit": "low", "dimension": "money", "evidence": "C", "grade": "C", "disputed": false, "cost": "Free. It takes a little time and attention to remember, and more night-time bathroom trips.", "plain": "Someone traced the \"eight glasses a day\" rule to its source and found not one supporting study. Food and other drinks already carry large amounts of water, so that much is unnecessary. Drink when thirsty; the colour of your urine is guide enough. People working in heat, exercising hard, with a history of kidney stones, and older people who feel less thirst should drink proactively.", "benefit_text": "A literature review traced the \"8x8\" claim — eight 8-ounce glasses a day — and concluded \"no scientific studies were found in support of 8x8\". It also noted that surveys of food and fluid intake in thousands of adults \"strongly suggest such large amounts are not needed\", because food and other drinks already provide large amounts of water.", "sources": "Valtin H (2002). \"Drink at least eight glasses of water a day.\" Really? Is there scientific evidence for \"8 x 8\"? Am J Physiol Regul Integr Comp Physiol. <https://doi.org/10.1152/ajpregu.00365.2002>", "notes": "What is ruled out is the \"must drink N glasses\" number, not drinking water. Drink when thirsty, check urine colour — that is enough. People working in heat, exercising hard, with a history of kidney stones, and older people who feel less thirst should drink proactively — a separate matter.", "urls": ["https://doi.org/10.1152/ajpregu.00365.2002"]}, {"id": "06-017", "chapter_num": 6, "chapter_title": "The blacklist (seems smart, isn't)", "chapter_file": "06-the-blacklist.md", "number": 17, "title": "Do not pay for fortune-telling, tarot or horoscopes to make decisions", "money": "0", "time": "low", "willpower": "no", "benefit": "mid", "dimension": "money", "evidence": "B", "grade": "B", "disputed": false, "cost": "Tens of dollars as entertainment is not counted here. The hard part is giving up a shortcut for decisions — afterwards you have to think for yourself.", "plain": "In a classic classroom experiment, every student got the identical \"personal analysis\", yet almost all rated it accurate. That is why fortune-telling feels accurate. Astrologers picking matches did about as well as blind guessing. And in Canada, taking money by deceit is fraud: over five thousand dollars can mean up to fourteen years.", "benefit_text": "Forer's classroom experiment: students got identical \"personal analyses\" after a personality test, yet rated them accurate — 34 of 39 scored it 4 or 5 out of 5, averaging about 4.26. The author's conclusion: \"accepted by the person or the analyst proves nothing about the interpretation's correctness\". This is the Barnum effect — it explains why fortune-telling always feels accurate. Astrology was tested double-blind, published in Nature: astrologers had to match star charts to one of three personality profiles. Of 116, they picked 40; pure guessing would pick 38.5; the astrologers had predicted 58.5. The data were \"consistent with chance, inconsistent with the astrological hypothesis\". In Canada, Criminal Code s. 380: fraud by deceit, falsehood or other fraudulent means — over $5,000 carries up to 14 years' imprisonment; the $5,000 line divides the more serious charge from the less.", "sources": "Forer BR (1949). The fallacy of personal validation: A classroom demonstration of gullibility. Journal of Abnormal and Social Psychology 44(1):118-123 <https://doi.org/10.1037/h0059240>; 「The data are consistent with chance, inconsistent with astrological hypothesis」）. <https://doi.org/10.1038/318419a0>; Carlson S (1985). A double-blind test of astrology. Nature 318:419-425 (full text via the second author's homepage). <https://muller.lbl.gov/papers/Astrology-Carlson.pdf>; Criminal Code, R.S.C. 1985, c. C-46, s. 380 (fraud). <https://laws-lois.justice.gc.ca/eng/acts/c-46/section-380.html>", "notes": "Both studies are single studies. Forer's paper never printed a mean; 4.26 is computed from the table's distribution. This item only says the predictions are not accurate; it evaluates no belief. Tens of dollars as entertainment is not counted — the loss is in deciding resignations, marriages and investments by it.", "urls": ["https://doi.org/10.1037/h0059240", "https://doi.org/10.1038/318419a0", "https://laws-lois.justice.gc.ca/eng/acts/c-46/section-380.html", "https://muller.lbl.gov/papers/Astrology-Carlson.pdf"]}, {"id": "06-018", "chapter_num": 6, "chapter_title": "The blacklist (seems smart, isn't)", "chapter_file": "06-the-blacklist.md", "number": 18, "title": "Do not buy blue-light glasses to \"protect your eyes\" — but treat sudden eye pain with redness as an emergency", "money": "0", "time": "low", "willpower": "no", "benefit": "mid", "dimension": "money", "evidence": "A", "grade": "A", "disputed": false, "cost": "Not buying saves the few hundred dollars of markup on the lenses.", "plain": "Blue-light lenses do nothing. Wearers reported no less eye fatigue and no clearer vision than plain lenses, and no evidence was found that they protect the retina. Screen-tired, dry eyes recover with rest. What really damages eyes fast is welding arcs, ultraviolet, lasers — and an acute glaucoma attack.", "benefit_text": "Cochrane's 2023 review took 17 randomized trials, 5 to 156 people each, follow-up from under a day to 5 weeks. The trials were too few and too different to pool. Versus lenses without blue-light filtering: self-reported eye fatigue \"probably no difference\" (low certainty) — in one 120-person trial the difference was 9.76 units with a 95% CI of -33.95 to 53.47, spanning zero. An objective fatigue measure \"probably little to no difference\" (low certainty). Best corrected vision \"probably unaffected\": 0.00 logMAR difference (95% CI -0.02 to 0.02; 1 study, 156 people, moderate certainty). Effects on daytime alertness unclear (2 trials, 42 people, very low certainty). No evidence found for macular protection.", "sources": "Singh S, Downie LE, Anderson AJ, Keller PR, White SJ, Ang M, Wolffsohn JS (2023). Blue-light filtering spectacle lenses for visual performance, sleep, and macular health in adults. Cochrane Database of Systematic Reviews, 8, CD013244. <https://doi.org/10.1002/14651858.CD013244.pub2>", "notes": "Screen use brings eye fatigue and dry eye — sore, dry, blurry after long sessions. Rest and blinking relieve it. It recovers; it is not \"ruining your eyes\". That sentence is ophthalmology consensus, not the Cochrane review's conclusion — hence C for that part. What causes irreversible eye damage in short order: welding arcs, ultraviolet, lasers. An arc-burned eye is \"welder's flash\", an occupational injury; it is blocked by goggles and face shields, not blue-light lenses. One exception to guard against after long nights at a screen: eye swelling and pain with redness, halos around lights, headache, nausea and vomiting — that is an acute angle-closure glaucoma attack, which can crush the optic nerve within days. Get to an eye emergency department that day. Triggers: dilated pupils in the dark and long hours looking down pushing the lens forward; high-risk people are over 50, farsighted, with shallow anterior chambers. Dry eye that drags on becomes a chronic condition needing a doctor, not a nap. Eyes that stay uncomfortable, or vision genuinely declining: get the cause checked at an eye clinic instead of swapping lenses. The price range is approximate 2026 Canadian retail, UNVERIFIED — see docs/verification/06-the-blacklist.md.", "urls": ["https://doi.org/10.1002/14651858.CD013244.pub2"]}, {"id": "06-019", "chapter_num": 6, "chapter_title": "The blacklist (seems smart, isn't)", "chapter_file": "06-the-blacklist.md", "number": 19, "title": "Do not donate blood as a health tonic — \"detox, lower lipids, prevent heart attacks\" has no trial backing, but donating too often really does cause iron deficiency", "money": "0", "time": "mid", "willpower": "some", "benefit": "low", "dimension": "death", "evidence": "A", "grade": "A", "disputed": false, "cost": "Free. One donation — forms, the draw, the rest afterwards — takes about an hour. For weeks after, the body is replacing the lost iron.", "plain": "\"Donating detoxes you, lowers lipids, prevents heart attacks\" does not hold up. People who keep donating are healthier to begin with; once that is accounted for, the mortality edge of each extra yearly donation shrank from nearly a fifth to seven and a half percent. In the one trial that actually randomized people, deaths did not differ. The other way round, donating too often genuinely leaves people tired and cold — from iron deficiency.", "benefit_text": "Finland's Kuopio study followed 2,862 men aged 42 to 60 for nearly 9 years. 153 had donated in the 24 months before baseline; 1 of them (0.7%) had an acute heart attack, versus 316 of 2,529 non-donors (12.5%). After adjusting for age and other coronary risk factors, donors' heart-attack risk was about 88% lower: RR 0.12 (95% CI 0.02–0.86). But this is follow-up only, no randomization. Scandinavia's SCANDAT database: 1,182,495 donors, 9,526,627 person-years, 15,401 deaths. Adjusting only for age and sex, each extra yearly donation came with 18.6% lower mortality (95% CI 16.8%–20.4%). After also accounting for \"only the persistently healthy keep being allowed to donate\", the edge shrank to 7.5% (5.7%–9.4%). The authors' conclusion: \"cannot be interpreted as firm evidence of a health benefit\". The FeAST randomized trial: 1,277 patients with symptomatic peripheral artery disease, one group bled regularly to reduce iron, one control. Total deaths 125 of 636 (20%) versus 148 of 641 (23%): HR 0.85 (95% CI 0.67–1.08, P = 0.17) — no difference. The INTERVAL randomized trial: 45,263 whole-blood donors in three interval groups (men 12/10/8 weeks, women 16/14/12). Shorter intervals collected clearly more blood over two years, with no difference in quality of life, physical activity or cognition — but more donation-related discomfort (fatigue, breathlessness, dizziness, restless legs at night) and lower haemoglobin and ferritin, with more deferrals for low haemoglobin (all P < 0.0001). Iron deficiency impairs heat production: 10 iron-deficient anaemic women, 8 iron-depleted non-anaemic women, 12 controls, 100 minutes in 28°C water — rectal temperature 36.0 vs 36.2°C (P = 0.001), oxygen use lower, thyroid hormones lower (P < 0.002).", "sources": "Salonen JT, Tuomainen TP, Salonen R, Lakka TA, Nyyssönen K (1998). Donation of blood is associated with reduced risk of myocardial infarction. The Kuopio Ischaemic Heart Disease Risk Factor Study. American Journal of Epidemiology, 148(5), 445-451. <https://doi.org/10.1093/oxfordjournals.aje.a009669>; Ullum H, Rostgaard K, Kamper-Jørgensen M, et al. (2015). Blood donation and blood donor mortality after adjustment for a healthy donor effect. Transfusion, 55(10), 2479-2485. <https://doi.org/10.1111/trf.13205>; Zacharski LR, Chow BK, Howes PS, et al. (2007). Reduction of iron stores and cardiovascular outcomes in patients with peripheral arterial disease: a randomized controlled trial. JAMA, 297(6), 603-610. <https://doi.org/10.1001/jama.297.6.603>; Di Angelantonio E, Thompson SG, Kaptoge S, et al. (2017). Efficiency and safety of varying the frequency of whole blood donation (INTERVAL): a randomised trial of 45 000 donors. Lancet, 390(10110), 2360-2371. <https://doi.org/10.1016/S0140-6736(17)31928-1>; Beard JL, Borel MJ, Derr J (1990). Impaired thermoregulation and thyroid function in iron-deficiency anemia. American Journal of Clinical Nutrition, 52(5), 813-819. <https://doi.org/10.1093/ajcn/52.5.813>; Canadian Blood Services. Am I eligible to donate blood? <https://www.blood.ca/en/blood/am-i-eligible-donate-blood>", "notes": "What is ruled out is donating \"for your own body\", not donating itself. Blood is for other people. Donate on the legal intervals: Canadian Blood Services allows whole blood every 56 days for men and every 84 days for women; Héma-Québec uses the same intervals. Do not squeeze the interval to chase a milestone or a group quota. Women, lighter people and heavy bleeders start with tighter iron; frequent donors can pay for one ferritin test themselves. If fatigue, feeling cold, or restless legs at night persist after donating, do not guess it is \"damaged vitality\" — get a blood count and ferritin checked. If iron deficiency is found, supplement as your doctor says and stretch the donation interval.", "urls": ["https://doi.org/10.1001/jama.297.6.603", "https://doi.org/10.1016/S0140-6736(17", "https://doi.org/10.1093/ajcn/52.5.813", "https://doi.org/10.1093/oxfordjournals.aje.a009669", "https://doi.org/10.1111/trf.13205", "https://www.blood.ca/en/blood/am-i-eligible-donate-blood"]}, {"id": "06-020", "chapter_num": 6, "chapter_title": "The blacklist (seems smart, isn't)", "chapter_file": "06-the-blacklist.md", "number": 20, "title": "Do not rely on a generic annual checkup package — do the few evidence-backed screenings for your age and sex", "money": "0", "time": "low", "willpower": "no", "benefit": "mid", "dimension": "money", "evidence": "A", "grade": "A", "disputed": false, "cost": "Not buying saves hundreds to thousands a year on packages. What it costs instead is remembering which tests, at which intervals.", "plain": "People who got regular general health checks died at the same rate as those who did not — same for cardiovascular and cancer deaths. The few evidence-backed screenings for your age and sex are enough.", "benefit_text": "Cochrane's 2019 update pooled 17 randomized trials. Total mortality (11 trials, 233,298 people, 21,535 deaths): general-check group versus control RR 1.00 (95% CI 0.97–1.03). Cardiovascular death (9 trials, 170,227 people, 6,237 deaths): RR 1.05 (0.94–1.16). Cancer death (8 trials, 139,290 people, 3,663 deaths): RR 1.01 (0.92–1.12). No difference anywhere. The authors' conclusion: general health checks are \"unlikely to be beneficial\".", "sources": "Krogsbøll LT, Jørgensen KJ, Gøtzsche PC (2019). General health checks in adults for reducing morbidity and mortality from disease. Cochrane Database of Systematic Reviews, 1, CD009009. <https://doi.org/10.1002/14651858.CD009009.pub3>", "notes": "What is ruled out is \"buying the annual package as disease prevention\", not all testing. Tests with a reason behind them get done as usual. If your employer pays for a checkup, going is fine — what is ruled out is buying the package yourself and adding items. People who already have a chronic condition, have symptoms, or whose doctor ordered tests are being treated, not screened — not covered here. The most common evidence-free items in packages are tumour markers and whole-body imaging — see item 9 in this chapter. Found high uric acid with no pain, or gallstones with no pain: see items 21 and 22 in this chapter. Also, most trials Cochrane pooled were done in high-income countries where care is easy to reach; do not copy the conclusion directly to places where medical resources are tight.", "urls": ["https://doi.org/10.1002/14651858.CD009009.pub3"]}, {"id": "06-021", "chapter_num": 6, "chapter_title": "The blacklist (seems smart, isn't)", "chapter_file": "06-the-blacklist.md", "number": 21, "title": "Do not start uric-acid-lowering drugs for high uric acid that never hurt", "money": "0", "time": "low", "willpower": "no", "benefit": "low", "dimension": "death", "evidence": "A", "grade": "A", "disputed": false, "cost": "Not starting saves years of drug costs and follow-up visits, and one unnecessary medication risk.", "plain": "For high uric acid with no gout attack and no tophi, the American College of Rheumatology conditionally recommends not starting uric-acid-lowering drugs. Twenty-four people treated for three years prevents one gout attack. Hoping it protects the kidneys also failed: drug takers' kidney function declined just as fast as placebo takers'. Treat it once it actually attacks.", "benefit_text": "The ACR's 2020 gout guideline on asymptomatic hyperuricaemia (uric acid > 6.8 mg/dL, no prior gout attack, no subcutaneous tophi): conditional recommendation against starting any urate-lowering drug (allopurinol, febuxostat, probenecid), high certainty. A footnote: by attributable risk, 24 patients must be treated for 3 years to prevent one gout attack. The CKD-FIX randomized trial: 363 people with stage 3–4 chronic kidney disease, no gout history but at risk of progression — 100–300 mg allopurinol a day versus placebo for 104 weeks. Kidney function (eGFR) changed -3.33 per year versus -3.23: difference -0.10 (-1.18 to 0.97), P = 0.85 — declining equally fast. Serious adverse events 46% versus 44%. The PERL randomized trial: 530 people with type 1 diabetes and early diabetic kidney disease, 3 years of allopurinol then a 2-month washout. Allopurinol did cut uric acid from 6.1 to 3.9 mg/dL — but after washout, kidney function differed by 0.001 mL/min/1.73 m² (95% CI -1.9 to 1.9, P = 0.99), and urinary albumin was 40% higher in the allopurinol group (0 to 80).", "sources": "FitzGerald JD, Dalbeth N, Mikuls T, et al. (2020). 2020 American College of Rheumatology Guideline for the Management of Gout. Arthritis Care & Research, 72(6), 744-760 <https://doi.org/10.1002/acr.24180>; Badve SV, Pascoe EM, Tiku A, et al. (2020). Effects of Allopurinol on the Progression of Chronic Kidney Disease. New England Journal of Medicine, 382(26), 2504-2513. <https://doi.org/10.1056/NEJMoa1915833>; Doria A, Galecki AT, Spino C, et al. (2020). Serum Urate Lowering with Allopurinol and Kidney Function in Type 1 Diabetes. New England Journal of Medicine, 382(26), 2493-2503. <https://doi.org/10.1056/NEJMoa1916624>", "notes": "What is ruled out is starting lifelong drugs with no attack — not that high uric acid does not matter. The same guideline lists exceptions worth starting for: a first attack together with stage 3+ chronic kidney disease; uric acid above 9 mg/dL (about 535 µmol/L); urinary stones. People who have already had attacks, have tophi, or show bone erosion on imaging should take the drugs. About 7.4% of Han Chinese carry the HLA-B*5801 gene type versus 0.7% of white people; carriers who take allopurinol are more likely to develop life-threatening severe rashes, and Asians' risk of hypersensitivity syndrome is 3 times white people's. This is a drug you could have skipped — no need to take the extra risk. Get the gene type tested before starting. The two kidney trials were in chronic-kidney-disease and type-1-diabetes patients; they do not generalize to \"high uric acid is harmless to kidneys\". They rule out only the one move: lowering uric acid with drugs to protect kidneys.", "urls": ["https://doi.org/10.1002/acr.24180", "https://doi.org/10.1056/NEJMoa1915833", "https://doi.org/10.1056/NEJMoa1916624"]}, {"id": "06-022", "chapter_num": 6, "chapter_title": "The blacklist (seems smart, isn't)", "chapter_file": "06-the-blacklist.md", "number": 22, "title": "Do not remove the gallbladder preventively for gallstones that never hurt", "money": "0", "time": "low", "willpower": "no", "benefit": "low", "dimension": "death", "evidence": "A", "grade": "A", "disputed": false, "cost": "Not operating saves one general-anaesthesia surgery and one hospital stay. What it costs instead is remembering which warning signs mean going to hospital that day.", "plain": "People with gallstones that never hurt mostly never hurt. Italy followed five hundred and eighty of them for nearly nine years: about four in five never developed symptoms. An American group of one hundred and thirty-five followed about four years: seven percent had surgery. Britain's guideline says it directly: incidentally found, symptom-free gallstones need no treatment before symptoms appear.", "benefit_text": "Italy's MICOL census ultrasounded 11,229 people aged 29 to 69; 856 had gallstones, 580 without symptoms, followed 8.7 years on average. Of the 580: 453 (78.1%) stayed symptom-free, 61 (10.5%) developed mild symptoms, 66 (11.4%) severe. 189 gallbladder removals in the follow-up, 41.3% of them in symptom-free people. The authors' conclusion: \"expectant management remains effective for most patients\". A US HMO followed 691 diagnosed gallstone patients for 78 ± 61.6 months; 135 symptom-free people were followed 58 ± 50.2 months (median 46.3): only 10% developed symptoms, 7% had surgery. Of 50 deaths in the whole group, only 2 were biliary — both in the symptomatic group. The authors' conclusion: patients with silent stones do not need surgery before symptoms appear. The UK's NICE guideline CG188 (1.2.1): for people with incidentally found, asymptomatic gallstones and a normal gallbladder and biliary tree, \"advise that they do not need treatment before symptoms appear\".", "sources": "Festi D, Reggiani ML, Attili AF, et al. (2010). Natural history of gallstone disease: Expectant management or active treatment? Results from a population-based cohort study. Journal of Gastroenterology and Hepatology, 25(4), 719-724. <https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1440-1746.2009.06146.x>; McSherry CK, Ferstenberg H, Calhoun WF, Lahman E, Virshup M (1985). The natural history of diagnosed gallstone disease in symptomatic and asymptomatic patients. Annals of Surgery, 202(1), 59-63. <https://doi.org/10.1097/00000658-198507000-00009>; National Institute for Health and Care Excellence (2014). Gallstone disease: diagnosis and management. NICE guideline CG188. <https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/cg188/chapter/Recommendations>", "notes": "This covers one situation only: stones in the gallbladder that never hurt. Stones in the common bile duct are a different matter — the same guideline (1.3.1) says duct stones get cleared and the gallbladder removed whether or not there are symptoms. Go to hospital that day, and do not tough it out on this item's conclusion, if: persistent colicky pain in the right upper or upper abdomen, fever with chills, yellowing eyes or skin, darkening urine. When a doctor who has seen the imaging recommends surgery, ask which reason it is based on — duct involvement, a polyp, an abnormal gallbladder wall — instead of arguing with this conclusion. It blocks only \"cutting just because a stone was found, for no other reason\". Both studies followed people forward without a control group — but they answer \"what happens if you leave it alone\", not \"which treatment is better\", which fits here. The follow-ups ran 4 to 9 years; with longer years, the symptomatic share rises.", "urls": ["https://doi.org/10.1097/00000658-198507000-00009", "https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1440-1746.2009.06146.x", "https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/cg188/chapter/Recommendations"]}, {"id": "06-023", "chapter_num": 6, "chapter_title": "The blacklist (seems smart, isn't)", "chapter_file": "06-the-blacklist.md", "number": 23, "title": "Do not buy crystals, bracelets or pixiu charms for \"luck\", \"wealth\" or \"nourishing the wearer\"", "money": "0", "time": "low", "willpower": "some", "benefit": "mid", "dimension": "money", "evidence": "B", "grade": "B", "disputed": false, "cost": "Nothing to spend. The only move is not counting \"luck\" or \"nourishing\" among the reasons to buy. The hard part is not following when everyone around you wears one and swears it works.", "plain": "There is no checkable evidence that wearing one changes luck, wealth, health. It feels accurate for the same reason fortune-telling does: the claims are vague enough that you fit them onto yourself. Buying it as decoration is fine.", "benefit_text": "Forer's classroom experiment: everyone got the same vague \"personal analysis\", yet almost all rated it accurate — the Barnum effect. The author's conclusion: \"accepted by the person proves nothing about the interpretation's correctness\". That is where the \"it really worked after I wore it\" feeling comes from.", "sources": "Forer BR (1949). The fallacy of personal validation: A classroom demonstration of gullibility. Journal of Abnormal and Social Psychology 44(1):118-123 <https://doi.org/10.1037/h0059240>", "notes": "One conclusion only: its claimed effects cannot be checked and show no predictive power. No belief or custom is evaluated here, and gift, commemorative or heirloom uses are not targeted. Two things to separate: a seller's efficacy claims have written rules behind them and can be complained about; \"luck\" claims have neither studies nor a way to be proven false — only your own budget discipline guards against them. Before buying, treat it as an ordinary ornament and ask: \"if it changed nothing, would I still pay this price?\" Paying for fortune-telling: see item 17 in this chapter. How to think about jewellery as spending: see the \"Don't waste your money\" chapter.", "urls": ["https://doi.org/10.1037/h0059240"]}, {"id": "06-024", "chapter_num": 6, "chapter_title": "The blacklist (seems smart, isn't)", "chapter_file": "06-the-blacklist.md", "number": 24, "title": "Do not expect shopping to fix your mood or your sense of status", "money": "0", "time": "low", "willpower": "yes", "benefit": "mid", "dimension": "money", "evidence": "B (disputed)", "grade": "B", "disputed": true, "cost": "Free. The hard part is not placing the order when you feel bad.", "plain": "The more people value money and possessions, the less happy they report being — but the link is weak, and no one can tell which causes which. Other research finds money spent on an experience brings more happiness than money spent on things. So when you feel bad, spending on an outing, on a meal with someone is more likely to help than filling a cart.", "benefit_text": "A meta-analysis of 259 independent samples, 753 effect sizes: the more materialistic people were, the lower their self-reported well-being. On the most-used multi-dimensional scale, r = -0.19 (corrected ρ = -0.24); on scales measuring only \"valuing money\", weaker: r = -0.08 to -0.11. By outcome: strongest with risky health and consumption behaviours and negative self-evaluation (r = -0.28 to -0.44); weakest with life satisfaction and negative affect (r = -0.13 to -0.15). A mediation analysis suggested the negative link may run through unmet psychological needs. Another study: in two surveys of different populations, respondents said money spent to gain a life experience made them happier than money spent on things. In follow-up lab experiments, recalling an experience purchase felt better than recalling a thing purchase.", "sources": "Dittmar H, Bond R, Hurst M, Kasser T (2014). The relationship between materialism and personal well-being: A meta-analysis. Journal of Personality and Social Psychology, 107(5), 879-924. <https://doi.org/10.1037/a0037409>; Van Boven L, Gilovich T (2003). To do or to have? That is the question. Journal of Personality and Social Psychology, 85(6), 1193-1202. <https://doi.org/10.1037/0022-3514.85.6.1193>", "notes": "Disputed. Most included studies asked one group of people once and checked whether two things moved together — no sense of which came first. The authors themselves say experiments and long-term studies are needed to see the direction. So it cannot be read as \"buying things makes people unhappy\" — only as \"counting on shopping to get happier is a long shot\". And the link is weak: r = -0.19 explains only a few percent of the difference; people vary hugely. Both studies used Western samples; no Canadian equivalent was found. This does not oppose spending on things you like — it opposes using shopping to regulate mood, because that makes your next bad mood's first reflex another order. Concrete saving moves: see the \"Don't waste your money\" chapter. What actually has evidence for bad moods: see the chapters on rest and on recovering from a major blow. For budgets added \"to rank one rung higher\": see item 28 in this chapter.", "urls": ["https://doi.org/10.1037/0022-3514.85.6.1193", "https://doi.org/10.1037/a0037409"]}, {"id": "06-025", "chapter_num": 6, "chapter_title": "The blacklist (seems smart, isn't)", "chapter_file": "06-the-blacklist.md", "number": 25, "title": "Do not believe \"willpower is a muscle that runs out\"", "money": "0", "time": "low", "willpower": "no", "benefit": "mid", "dimension": "time", "evidence": "A", "grade": "A", "disputed": false, "cost": "Free. Only one sentence needs changing.", "plain": "The popular story: do one self-control task first, and you are more likely to break next — so willpower must be spent sparingly. Twenty-three labs and over two thousand people redid the experiment on the same protocol, and the result was near zero. Stop explaining yourself with \"today's willpower is spent\", and stop paying for courses that teach you to hoard it.", "benefit_text": "A multi-lab pre-registered replication, 23 labs, 2,141 people, redid the sequential-task setup on a standardized protocol: do a self-control-consuming task first, then a second task needing self-control. The pooled result showed the depletion effect was tiny, with a 95% CI including zero (d = 0.04, 95% CI -0.07 to 0.15). The authors then discuss whether the \"self-control is a limited resource\" model still holds.", "sources": "Hagger et al. (2016). A Multilab Preregistered Replication of the Ego-Depletion Effect. Perspectives on Psychological Science. <https://doi.org/10.1177/1745691616652873>", "notes": "What is ruled out is the \"self-control is a resource that gets used up\" model — not that people get tired. Too little sleep or too long a stretch still degrades performance; that is handled by the sleep and work-hours items in the \"Don't waste your energy\" chapter. The moves with real trial backing are changing the environment and changing the wording: see item 13 in the \"Don't waste your time\" chapter (move what tempts you far away) and item 1 in the \"Don't waste your time\" chapter (write it as \"at what time, where, doing what when X happens\"). This section otherwise counts money; here it counts time and energy — the two are not converted into each other.", "urls": ["https://doi.org/10.1177/1745691616652873"]}, {"id": "06-026", "chapter_num": 6, "chapter_title": "The blacklist (seems smart, isn't)", "chapter_file": "06-the-blacklist.md", "number": 26, "title": "Do not expect skipping breakfast or 16:8 fasting to control your weight — pick eating hours you can keep", "money": "0", "time": "low", "willpower": "no", "benefit": "low", "dimension": "death", "evidence": "A (disputed)", "grade": "A", "disputed": true, "cost": "Free. What is saved is the daily wrestling with the clock.", "plain": "The popular story says skipping breakfast makes you binge at lunch and dinner, eating more in total. Trials say otherwise: skippers ate over two hundred kilocalories less a day and weighed slightly less. Sixteen-eight versus plain eating less showed no weight difference at a year. Long-term skippers did have about a sixth more cardiovascular disease — but total mortality showed no difference.", "benefit_text": "First the \"skipping breakfast makes you eat more later\" claim. A meta-analysis pooled 13 randomized trials. The breakfast group ate 259.79 kcal more per day than the skippers (95% CI 78.87–440.71) and weighed 0.44 kg more (0.07–0.82). Skippers did not make it up at lunch and dinner. These trials were short: 7 weeks on average for weight, 2 for intake. Next, time-restricted eating like 16:8. One trial, 116 overweight adults, 12 weeks: eating only noon to 8 p.m. versus three regular meals — weight difference -0.26 kg (-1.30 to 0.78), not significant. Another, in Guangzhou, 139 obese patients, 12 months: time-restricted eating plus calorie control versus calorie control alone — 1.8 kg more lost (-4.0 to 0.4), also not significant. Finally, is long-term skipping harmful? In a 2.38-million-person pooled analysis, skippers' cardiovascular-disease risk was about 17% higher (OR 1.17, 1.09–1.26) and cardiovascular death about 49% higher (OR 1.49, 1.20–1.84). But these are record-only, unrandomized studies. In the original study followed 17 to 23 years, total mortality HR was 1.19 with a CI of 0.99 to 1.42 — crossing 1, no difference.", "sources": "Sievert K, Hussain SM, Page MJ, et al. (2019). Effect of breakfast on weight and energy intake: systematic review and meta-analysis of randomised controlled trials. BMJ. <https://doi.org/10.1136/bmj.l42>; Lowe DA, Wu N, Rohdin-Bibby L, et al. (2020). Effects of Time-Restricted Eating on Weight Loss and Other Metabolic Parameters in Women and Men With Overweight and Obesity. JAMA Internal Medicine. <https://doi.org/10.1001/jamainternmed.2020.4153>; Liu D, Huang Y, Huang C, et al. (2022). Calorie Restriction with or without Time-Restricted Eating in Weight Loss. New England Journal of Medicine. <https://doi.org/10.1056/NEJMoa2114833>; Zhang H, Zhang S, Liu Y, et al. (2025). The association between skipping breakfast and cardiovascular disease: a meta analysis. Frontiers in Cardiovascular Medicine. <https://doi.org/10.3389/fcvm.2025.1565806>; Rong S, Snetselaar LG, Xu G, et al. (2019). Association of Skipping Breakfast With Cardiovascular and All-Cause Mortality. Journal of the American College of Cardiology. <https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jacc.2019.01.065>; Fernandes-Alves D, Teixeira GP, Guimarães KC, Crispim CA (2026). Systematic Review and Meta-analysis of Randomized Clinical Trials Comparing Time-Restricted Eating With and Without Caloric Restriction for Weight Loss. Nutrition Reviews. <https://doi.org/10.1093/nutrit/nuaf053>; Sun T, Zhang L, Lu Y, et al. (2024). Non-linear relationship between the first meal time of the day and gallstone incidence in American adults. Frontiers in Nutrition. <https://doi.org/10.3389/fnut.2024.1521707>; Liu T, Wang Y, Wang X, et al. (2023). Habitually Skipping Breakfast Is Associated with the Risk of Gastrointestinal Cancers: Evidence from the Kailuan Cohort Study. Journal of General Internal Medicine. <https://doi.org/10.1007/s11606-023-08094-7>", "notes": "Disputed. The 17% cardiovascular figure comes from record-only, unrandomized studies. Skippers were already more likely to smoke, drink, sit long hours and earn less — not fully adjustable. So how much of the 17% is breakfast itself is unclear. The total-mortality figure crossed 1 to begin with. 16:8 is also disputed: pooling 30 trials, 1,341 people, with both groups eating the same amount, the time-restricted group lost 1.46 kg more (-2.65 to -0.26) — at the cost of 0.41 kg more lean mass. But most of those trials were short; the longest, strictest 12-month trial said no difference. Long fasting also touches the gallbladder: in a 6,547-person cross-sectional survey, each hour later the day's first meal came, gallstones were about 5% more common (OR 1.05, 1.02–1.08); first meals between 9 a.m. and 2 p.m. ran about 49% higher than earlier ones (OR 1.49, 1.24–1.77). The mechanism: bile sitting longer in the gallbladder crystallizes more easily. But that is a same-time-point survey — it could also be gallbladder trouble changing meal habits first. A Chinese cohort followed 5.6 years, 369 digestive-tract cancers: skippers' colorectal-cancer risk 2.32 times (1.34–4.01), gallbladder and bile-duct cancer 5.43 times (1.34–21.93) — the second CI stretches to 21.93, meaning too few events; a lead only. Taken together: for weight control, meal timing matters little — do not spend willpower on it. On whether never eating breakfast long-term is fine, all existing evidence is associational and cannot prove cause, but it leans consistently unfavourable — eat normally to be safe, just do not expect it to lose the weight. Gastritis and ulcers are not caused by an empty stomach — the main causes are H. pylori and long-term painkiller use; see the \"Don't die young\" chapter. Gallstones found on a checkup with no pain: see item 22 in this chapter. What actually has evidence for weight control is what and how much you eat — see the \"Don't die slowly\" chapter. People with diabetes, on glucose-lowering drugs, or pregnant: not applicable — skipping a meal can mean low blood sugar; follow your doctor. Extreme fasting and vomiting are a different matter.", "urls": ["https://doi.org/10.1001/jamainternmed.2020.4153", "https://doi.org/10.1007/s11606-023-08094-7", "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jacc.2019.01.065", "https://doi.org/10.1056/NEJMoa2114833", "https://doi.org/10.1093/nutrit/nuaf053", "https://doi.org/10.1136/bmj.l42", "https://doi.org/10.3389/fcvm.2025.1565806", "https://doi.org/10.3389/fnut.2024.1521707"]}, {"id": "06-027", "chapter_num": 6, "chapter_title": "The blacklist (seems smart, isn't)", "chapter_file": "06-the-blacklist.md", "number": 27, "title": "Do not believe \"masturbation harms the body\" — watch whether it costs you sleep, work or relationships", "money": "0", "time": "low", "willpower": "no", "benefit": "low", "dimension": "death", "evidence": "B (disputed)", "grade": "B", "disputed": true, "cost": "Free. What is saved is the money for \"kidney-tonifying\" remedies and \"quit porn\" courses, plus the long-running anxiety of self-blame.", "plain": "No study says masturbation harms the body. A large US study instead saw men who ejaculated more often had about a fifth less prostate cancer. A British study saw higher risk in men who were frequent in their twenties and thirties — so neither side is a reason to treat it as exercise. Many people who feel \"addicted to porn\" feel that way mostly because it clashes with their own morals.", "benefit_text": "First the body. A US study followed 31,925 men to 2010, each self-reporting monthly ejaculation frequency. Versus 4 to 7 times a month: 21+ times a month came with prostate-cancer HR 0.81 (95% CI 0.72–0.92, about 19% lower) by frequency at ages 20 to 29, and HR 0.78 (0.69–0.89, about 22% lower) by frequency at ages 40 to 49. The drop was mostly in low-risk prostate cancer. The other side: a British case-control study of 431 patients diagnosed before 60 and 409 controls — frequent masturbation in the twenties and thirties ran with higher risk, frequent in the fifties with lower. Next, pornography. A systematic review with meta-analysis: many people who feel \"addicted\" feel so more because the behaviour clashes with what they believe is right, not only because they watch a lot.", "sources": "Rider JR, Wilson KM, Sinnott JA, et al. (2016). Ejaculation Frequency and Risk of Prostate Cancer: Updated Results with an Additional Decade of Follow-up. European Urology, 70(6), 974–982. <https://doi.org/10.1016/j.eururo.2016.03.027>; Dimitropoulou P, Lophatananon A, Easton D, et al. (2009). Sexual activity and prostate cancer risk in men diagnosed at a younger age. BJU International, 103(2), 178–185. <https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1464-410x.2008.08030.x>; Grubbs JB et al. (2018). Pornography Problems Due to Moral Incongruence: An Integrative Model with a Systematic Review and Meta-Analysis. Archives of Sexual Behavior. <https://doi.org/10.1007/s10508-018-1248-x>", "notes": "Disputed. Both prostate-cancer studies are record-only, unrandomized, with self-recalled frequency — so the conclusion only reaches \"no evidence of harm\", not advice to do more. The British study's authors themselves say the fifties pattern may be illness reducing frequency first. The porn review's abstract gave no effect size, so only the direction is written here. The benefit is graded low because the drop was mostly in low-risk prostate cancer, not deaths. The real signals for seeing a psychiatrist or psychologist: staying up for it, missing work, harming the partner relationship, wanting to stop and failing. Staying up for it: see item 3 in the \"Don't waste your energy\" chapter. Worried porn caused erectile problems: check cardiovascular causes first — see the \"Don't die young\" chapter.", "urls": ["https://doi.org/10.1007/s10508-018-1248-x", "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.eururo.2016.03.027", "https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1464-410x.2008.08030.x"]}, {"id": "06-028", "chapter_num": 6, "chapter_title": "The blacklist (seems smart, isn't)", "chapter_file": "06-the-blacklist.md", "number": 28, "title": "Do not spend extra to \"move one rung up\" among the people around you — bigger house, better car, better circle", "money": "0", "time": "low", "willpower": "some", "benefit": "mid", "dimension": "money", "evidence": "B", "grade": "B", "disputed": false, "cost": "Nothing to spend. The only move is asking, before adding to the budget: does this extra buy the thing itself, or my position in this group? The hard part is not following when everyone around you upgrades.", "plain": "Comparing people with the same income, the more the neighbours earn, the lower the person's reported happiness. This only happens with neighbours of the same education level whom you actually see. So money spent to move into a place where everyone is better off buys you one rung down. But when incomes rise together, people still feel better off — the absolute level still counts.", "benefit_text": "The US National Survey of Families and Households revisited the same people twice; the author matched the data to local average incomes (PUMA, estimated from the 1990 census 5% sample). After removing own income and personal characteristics: the higher the neighbours' income, the lower the self-reported happiness. The effect was large, stable under a different computation, and highly significant — the author's wording: a rise in neighbours' income and an equal-sized fall in own income cut happiness by a similar amount. With individual fixed effects (same person compared with themselves over time), the direction and size held, significance falling to the 10% level. With state and survey-wave fixed effects, the result barely changed — so it is not \"people who like poor places are happier anyway\", nor some unmeasured state-level factor. The effect was stronger in people who actually interact with neighbours, not significant in those who socialize outside the community. Renters and owners did not differ — which argues against \"expensive places leave less to spend\". Happiness fell only with same-education neighbours' income, barely reacting to differently-educated neighbours'. It ran mostly through satisfaction with one's own material situation, not through satisfaction with family life. Finally, if own and neighbours' incomes rose by the same proportion, happiness still rose.", "sources": "Luttmer EFP (2005). Neighbors as Negatives: Relative Earnings and Well-Being. The Quarterly Journal of Economics, 120(3), 963–1002. <https://doi.org/10.1093/qje/120.3.963>; Luttmer EFP (2004). Neighbors as Negatives: Relative Earnings and Well-Being. NBER Working Paper No. 10667 <https://www.nber.org/papers/w10667>", "notes": "The B grade: the data come from repeated questionnaires of the same people, a single study, and the sample is American; the outcome is self-reported happiness, not money. The author did use statistical means to rule out several confounders — same person over time, same state and wave — but it is still not a randomized trial. The benefit is graded \"mid\" following item 24 in this chapter's yardstick. The money saved can genuinely reach five figures — but \"how much happiness that saving buys\" has no number behind it, so it is not graded \"high\" on five-figure savings. This does not oppose spending on a bigger house, a quieter neighbourhood or a shorter commute — those buy the thing itself; see item 18 in the \"Don't waste your time\" chapter. What is blocked is the one reason: \"the colleagues all upgraded, I can't look worse\". Shopping to fix mood: see item 24 in this chapter. Comparing upward online: see item 6 in the \"Don't waste your energy\" chapter.", "urls": ["https://doi.org/10.1093/qje/120.3.963", "https://www.nber.org/papers/w10667"]}, {"id": "07-001", "chapter_num": 7, "chapter_title": "When You're Broke", "chapter_file": "07-when-youre-broke.md", "number": 1, "title": "EI regular benefits: 55% of pay, up to $729 a week", "money": "0", "time": "low", "willpower": "no", "benefit": "mid", "dimension": "money", "evidence": "B", "grade": "B", "disputed": false, "cost": "No money. Apply online through Service Canada as soon as you stop working; your employer must issue the Record of Employment.", "plain": "Losing a job you did not quit can mean 55% of your average weekly pay, up to $729 a week in 2026, for 14 to 45 weeks. You need 420 to 700 insured hours in the last 52 weeks. Quitting without a strong reason usually disqualifies you.", "benefit_text": "EI regular benefits (2026): 55% of average insurable weekly earnings; maximum yearly insurable earnings $68,900; maximum $729/week; 14 to 45 weeks depending on regional unemployment and hours worked; 420 to 700 insurable hours in the last 52 weeks. A full 45-week claim at the maximum pays $32,805 (2026 dollars).", "sources": "https://www.canada.ca/en/services/benefits/ei/ei-regular-benefit/benefit-amount.html ; https://www.canada.ca/en/services/benefits/ei/ei-regular-benefit/eligibility.html", "notes": "Quitting without just cause or dismissal for misconduct disqualifies you. Benefits are taxable. Only the last 52 weeks (or since your last claim) count toward the hours requirement; older gaps do not disqualify you. Quebec trap: this item is regular EI only — for maternity and parental benefits, Quebec residents get QPIP, not EI; the two are mutually exclusive. Benefit graded mid: a maximum single claim is $32,805, inside $5,000–$50,000. EI: 1-800-206-7218.", "urls": ["https://www.canada.ca/en/services/benefits/ei/ei-regular-benefit/benefit-amount.html", "https://www.canada.ca/en/services/benefits/ei/ei-regular-benefit/eligibility.html"]}, {"id": "07-002", "chapter_num": 7, "chapter_title": "When You're Broke", "chapter_file": "07-when-youre-broke.md", "number": 2, "title": "Ontario Works: up to $733 a month for a single person", "money": "0", "time": "mid", "willpower": "some", "benefit": "mid", "dimension": "money", "evidence": "B", "grade": "B", "disputed": false, "cost": "Ontario. No money. Apply through Ontario Works; bring ID, bank statements and tax documents for the interview; most recipients must take part in employment activities.", "plain": "Ontario's last-resort income support pays up to $733 a month for a single person in 2026, covering basic needs and shelter. That is $8,796 over a full year. You must be at least 16, live in Ontario, and be in financial need.", "benefit_text": "Ontario Works (2026): up to $733/month for basic needs and shelter for a single person; a full year at the maximum is $8,796 (2026 dollars). Eligibility: 16 or older, Ontario resident, in financial need, assets within limits; employment activities required in most cases.", "sources": "http://www.ontario.ca/page/ontario-works", "notes": "Ontario only. Quebec's equivalent (aide sociale) 2026 amounts are UNVERIFIED at write time — see docs/verification/07-when-youre-broke.md #1. In a crisis, ask your worker about emergency assistance (amounts UNVERIFIED, money.md #13) and shelter referrals (referral channels UNVERIFIED at write time). Benefit graded mid: a year at the maximum is $8,796, inside $5,000–$50,000.", "urls": ["http://www.ontario.ca/page/ontario-works"]}, {"id": "07-003", "chapter_num": 7, "chapter_title": "When You're Broke", "chapter_file": "07-when-youre-broke.md", "number": 3, "title": "Rent-geared-to-income housing: join the waitlist now", "money": "0", "time": "mid", "willpower": "no", "benefit": "mid", "dimension": "money", "evidence": "B", "grade": "B", "disputed": false, "cost": "Ontario. No money to apply. Applications go through your local Service Manager; waits are measured in years, so apply as early as you can.", "plain": "Rent-geared-to-income housing sets your rent from your income instead of the market. Ontario runs it through 47 local Service Managers, with a waitlist in each area. The wait is long, which is exactly why the application belongs at the top of your list, not the bottom.", "benefit_text": "Ontario (2026): rent-geared-to-income (RGI) community housing and the portable Canada-Ontario Housing Benefit (COHB), delivered with the federal government through 47 Service Managers. Applying is free; placement comes from a local waitlist.", "sources": "http://www.ontario.ca/document/ontarios-third-action-plan-under-national-housing-strategy-2025-2028/nhs-programs ; https://tenantrights.ca/ontario/affordable-housing-and-rent-subsidies-for-ontario-tenants", "notes": "Ontario only; Quebec runs its own low-rent housing programs (not verified at write time). The tenantrights.ca link is a secondary how-to page, not a government source; no ontario.ca application page exists — apply through your local Service Manager. Benefit graded mid by judgment: a year of income-geared rent typically saves a low-income household well over $5,000 versus market rent; exact savings depend on local rents.", "urls": ["http://www.ontario.ca/document/ontarios-third-action-plan-under-national-housing-strategy-2025-2028/nhs-programs", "https://tenantrights.ca/ontario/affordable-housing-and-rent-subsidies-for-ontario-tenants"]}, {"id": "07-004", "chapter_num": 7, "chapter_title": "When You're Broke", "chapter_file": "07-when-youre-broke.md", "number": 4, "title": "Canada Workers Benefit: up to $1,665 single, $2,869 family", "money": "0", "time": "low", "willpower": "no", "benefit": "low", "dimension": "money", "evidence": "B", "grade": "B", "disputed": false, "cost": "No money. There is no separate application: the CRA calculates the benefit from your tax return, so you must file every year.", "plain": "If you work but earn little, the Canada Workers Benefit tops up your pay through your tax return: up to $1,665 a year for a single person and up to $2,869 for a family in 2026. It is refundable, so it can pay out even when you owe no tax.", "benefit_text": "CWB (July 2026–June 2027): up to $1,665 for a single person and up to $2,869 for a family (for example, a family earning less than $49,000 per year). Refundable: paid even if you owe no tax.", "sources": "https://www.newswire.ca/news-releases/tax-tip-tax-filing-highlights-for-first-nations-inuit-and-metis-2026-872575980.html", "notes": "2026 maximums corroborated via a CRA 2026 tax tip republished on newswire.ca; the primary CRA CWB page stating the 2026 maximums was not captured at write time — see docs/verification/07-when-youre-broke.md #2. The $860 disability supplement is UNVERIFIED. Quebec uses a separate CWB calculation via Revenu Québec (details UNVERIFIED, money.md #7). Benefit graded low: under $5,000 per year.", "urls": ["https://www.newswire.ca/news-releases/tax-tip-tax-filing-highlights-for-first-nations-inuit-and-metis-2026-872575980.html"]}, {"id": "07-005", "chapter_num": 7, "chapter_title": "When You're Broke", "chapter_file": "07-when-youre-broke.md", "number": 5, "title": "Canada Groceries and Essentials Benefit: the renamed grocery credit", "money": "0", "time": "low", "willpower": "no", "benefit": "low", "dimension": "money", "evidence": "B", "grade": "B", "disputed": false, "cost": "No money. There is no application: when you file your taxes, the CRA considers you automatically.", "plain": "The old GST/HST credit is now called the Canada Groceries and Essentials Benefit. For July 2026 to June 2027 it pays up to $679 a year for a single person, up to $890 for a couple, plus $234 per child under 19. Filing your return is the whole application.", "benefit_text": "CGEB (July 2026–June 2027, based on the 2025 tax return): up to $679 for a single person, $890 for a married or common-law partner, $234 for each child under 19. Tax-free quarterly payments. The GST/HST credit name is retired as of July 2026.", "sources": "https://www.canada.ca/en/revenue-agency/services/child-family-benefits/canada-groceries-essentials-benefit.html", "notes": "Federal, via the CRA. Never write \"GST/HST credit\" as current — the name changed in July 2026. Benefit graded low: under $5,000 per year.", "urls": ["https://www.canada.ca/en/revenue-agency/services/child-family-benefits/canada-groceries-essentials-benefit.html"]}, {"id": "07-006", "chapter_num": 7, "chapter_title": "When You're Broke", "chapter_file": "07-when-youre-broke.md", "number": 6, "title": "Legal aid: a free lawyer if you qualify financially", "money": "0", "time": "low", "willpower": "no", "benefit": "mid", "dimension": "money", "evidence": "B", "grade": "B", "disputed": false, "cost": "Ontario. No money if you qualify financially. Apply to Legal Aid Ontario; a certificate pays a private lawyer for a set number of hours.", "plain": "If a legal problem could cost you thousands and you cannot pay, legal aid can cover a lawyer for you. In Ontario, families of up to four qualify for duty counsel and criminal certificates with income under $45,440 and assets under $15,000 through spring 2028. Quebec runs its own program with its own thresholds.", "benefit_text": "Legal Aid Ontario (2025-03-31 to spring 2028): income under $45,440 for families of up to four and assets under $15,000 for duty counsel and criminal certificate services. Thresholds vary by matter type (family, immigration, criminal). Quebec: Commission des services juridiques, single-person threshold $29,302/year (2025 indexation).", "sources": "https://www.legalaid.on.ca/news/legal-aid-ontario-raising-financial-eligibility-for-duty-counsel-and-criminal-certificate-services/ ; https://www.legalaid.on.ca/will-legal-aid-pay-for-my-lawyer/", "notes": "legalaid.on.ca is Legal Aid Ontario, a provincial agency — cited as the administering body (not an ontario.ca page). The $45,440/$15,000 figures apply to duty counsel and criminal certificates; family and immigration certificate thresholds are lower — check the second link for your matter type. Benefit graded mid by judgment: a certificate for a contested matter routinely replaces several thousand dollars in private legal fees.", "urls": ["https://www.legalaid.on.ca/news/legal-aid-ontario-raising-financial-eligibility-for-duty-counsel-and-criminal-certificate-services/", "https://www.legalaid.on.ca/will-legal-aid-pay-for-my-lawyer/"]}, {"id": "07-007", "chapter_num": 7, "chapter_title": "When You're Broke", "chapter_file": "07-when-youre-broke.md", "number": 7, "title": "File a tax return every year, even with zero income", "money": "0", "time": "low", "willpower": "no", "benefit": "mid", "dimension": "money", "evidence": "B", "grade": "B", "disputed": false, "cost": "No money for the filing itself with free software or a volunteer tax clinic. The real cost is an hour of paperwork once a year.", "plain": "The CRA cannot pay you benefits without a tax return on file. No return means no Canada Workers Benefit, no Groceries and Essentials Benefit, no Child Benefit. In 2026 you can earn up to $16,452 without owing federal tax, but you still need to file to get paid.", "benefit_text": "CRA (2026): federal basic personal amount $16,452 — earn up to $16,452 in 2026 before owing federal income tax; lowest marginal rate 14% on the first $58,523. Benefit payments (CWB, CGEB, CCB) are calculated from the filed return; without a return on file, no payment.", "sources": "https://www.canada.ca/content/dam/fin/publications/nrtc-cinr/nrtc-cinr-eng.pdf ; https://www.newswire.ca/news-releases/tax-tip-tax-filing-highlights-for-first-nations-inuit-and-metis-2026-872575980.html", "notes": "Quebec residents file twice: federal T1 to the CRA and provincial TP-1 to Revenu Québec. Benefit graded mid by judgment: one filed return unlocks several benefit streams worth thousands per year, compounding past $5,000 within a few years.", "urls": ["https://www.canada.ca/content/dam/fin/publications/nrtc-cinr/nrtc-cinr-eng.pdf", "https://www.newswire.ca/news-releases/tax-tip-tax-filing-highlights-for-first-nations-inuit-and-metis-2026-872575980.html"]}]);
